    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a clear focus on speed and favorable park factors, prioritizing high-efficiency matchups to maximize our 5x5 production. Our strongest play is **Brice Turang** (73.37), who sits atop our projections thanks to a perfect storm: a +14% park adjustment and an advantageous look at Freeland, whose slow delivery significantly elevates the stolen base ceiling\u2014a critical factor for our team strategy. **Corbin Carroll** (72.42) remains a must-start staple in the OF2 slot, leveraging a dynamic platoon advantage against Cavalli to anchor our offense. Meanwhile, **Josh Naylor** (67.78) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (66.19) provide reliable power production against favorable xERA matchups, ensuring that despite the focus on speed, our HR and RBI foundations remain intact.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Austin Riley** (43.33). While **Austin Riley** possesses the pedigree to produce regardless of matchup, the combination of a tough outing against Montgomery and a high elite-catcher defensive risk tipped the optimizer toward favoring **Kyle Karros** (49.07) for his superior park-adjusted outlook. We are also closely monitoring the marginal floor of **Zach McKinstry** (43.90); while he remains in the lineup, his low score is suppressed by a negative platoon and a bottom-order placement against the formidable arm of Castillo. With **Mickey Moniak** sidelined on the IL, we are leaning heavily on **James Wood** (60.03) to provide high-ceiling performance from the top of the order, though we remain wary of the strikeout risk inherent in his matchup against Soroka. Owners should keep a close eye on the late-breaking lineup news for **Dalton Rushing** and **Jose Caballero**, as any volatility in their batting positions could further compress their currently marginal efficiency scores.",
